WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.003
In this lesson you'll learn how your computer

2
00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:05.931
finds the right program when you type

3
00:00:05.931 --> 00:00:08.740
a name like python, and about the hidden

4
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.772
settings every program carries with it.

5
00:00:12.235 --> 00:00:13.640
Every program starts

6
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.101
with a copy of its parent's settings,

7
00:00:16.101 --> 00:00:18.330
called its environment.

8
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.281
One of those settings, path,

9
00:00:20.281 --> 00:00:22.452
is an ordered list of folders,

10
00:00:22.452 --> 00:00:24.727
and the first match wins.

11
00:00:24.727 --> 00:00:28.396
So which program runs, and which keys it can see,

12
00:00:28.396 --> 00:00:31.196
depends on who started it, and when.

13
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.203
"Not recognized as a command."

14
00:00:34.619 --> 00:00:36.209
The wrong Python.

15
00:00:36.209 --> 00:00:38.671
An AI tool billing the wrong account.

16
00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:41.353
That's one idea, not three problems.

17
00:00:41.353 --> 00:00:45.789
And agents now work inside it too.

18
00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:49.095
This lesson sits in the first district,

19
00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:52.439
The Machine, which answers one question:

20
00:00:52.439 --> 00:00:54.772
what's on your computer?

21
00:00:54.772 --> 00:00:57.605
Module one, lesson five.

22
00:00:57.605 --> 00:01:01.111
The last two lessons showed where files live,

23
00:01:01.111 --> 00:01:03.572
and what hides in the back room.

24
00:01:03.572 --> 00:01:05.929
This one shows what every running

25
00:01:05.929 --> 00:01:08.647
program carries out the door.

26
00:01:09.098 --> 00:01:12.268
Picture every running program as a courier.

27
00:01:12.268 --> 00:01:15.913
Another program sends it out: File Explorer,

28
00:01:15.913 --> 00:01:19.268
a terminal, an editor, or an agent.

29
00:01:19.268 --> 00:01:21.277
That sender is its parent.

30
00:01:21.277 --> 00:01:24.481
At the door, the parent copies its own

31
00:01:24.481 --> 00:01:29.253
satchel of labelled cards and hands over the copy.

32
00:01:29.253 --> 00:01:30.762
Then the door closes,

33
00:01:30.762 --> 00:01:33.467
and the satchel belongs to the courier.

34
00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:36.927
Nothing you change at home reaches it on the road.

35
00:01:36.927 --> 00:01:39.109
And nothing the courier changes

36
00:01:39.109 --> 00:01:41.025
ever comes back home.

37
00:01:41.025 --> 00:01:43.463
The you-are-here dot from two lessons

38
00:01:43.463 --> 00:01:46.646
ago travels the same way, as a copy.

39
00:01:47.097 --> 00:01:49.465
Each card is an environment variable:

40
00:01:49.465 --> 00:01:53.099
a name, and a value that is plain text.

41
00:01:53.099 --> 00:01:55.886
One card says where your home folder is.

42
00:01:55.886 --> 00:01:58.742
Another says where temporary files go.

43
00:01:58.742 --> 00:02:01.703
And one long card is called path.

44
00:02:01.703 --> 00:02:02.701
On Windows,

45
00:02:02.701 --> 00:02:06.161
the master copy sits in two drawers at home,

46
00:02:06.161 --> 00:02:08.924
one for you and one for the whole system,

47
00:02:08.924 --> 00:02:11.722
and new programs get both combined.

48
00:02:11.722 --> 00:02:13.487
On a Mac or on Linux,

49
00:02:13.487 --> 00:02:15.925
the shell's startup files write it.

50
00:02:15.925 --> 00:02:18.769
The screen shows how to peek inside;

51
00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:20.559
you don't need it yet.

52
00:02:21.008 --> 00:02:24.270
Path is one card holding a list of folders.

53
00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:28.195
Picture them as numbered streets.

54
00:02:28.195 --> 00:02:31.062
On Windows, semicolons separate them;

55
00:02:31.062 --> 00:02:33.953
on a Mac and Linux, colons.

56
00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:36.821
When you type a bare name like python,

57
00:02:36.821 --> 00:02:39.212
the courier walks the streets in order,

58
00:02:39.212 --> 00:02:42.022
and goes into the first shop with that name.

59
00:02:42.022 --> 00:02:43.949
First match wins.

60
00:02:43.949 --> 00:02:47.003
Shops further down are never visited.

61
00:02:47.003 --> 00:02:50.463
On Windows, it also tries the endings

62
00:02:50.463 --> 00:02:52.738
Windows counts as programs,

63
00:02:52.738 --> 00:02:56.500
so python finds python.exe.

64
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.402
And on Windows, the system's list comes first,

65
00:02:59.402 --> 00:03:01.921
and your own list is added after it.

66
00:03:01.921 --> 00:03:04.545
Shells check their own built-in commands

67
00:03:04.545 --> 00:03:07.007
and aliases before path.

68
00:03:07.007 --> 00:03:08.632
Old Command Prompt

69
00:03:08.632 --> 00:03:11.534
even looks in the current folder first;

70
00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:14.810
PowerShell deliberately doesn't.

71
00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:17.374
Not recognized means one thing:

72
00:03:17.374 --> 00:03:19.324
the courier walked every street

73
00:03:19.324 --> 00:03:21.612
and found no shop with that name.

74
00:03:21.612 --> 00:03:24.038
Usually, it isn't installed.

75
00:03:24.038 --> 00:03:25.199
Or it's installed,

76
00:03:25.199 --> 00:03:27.544
but its folder isn't on the list.

77
00:03:27.544 --> 00:03:28.786
Or it's on the list,

78
00:03:28.786 --> 00:03:31.642
but this terminal left home before the change.

79
00:03:31.642 --> 00:03:33.198
Or it's a typo.

80
00:03:33.198 --> 00:03:35.787
The message comes in three outfits.

81
00:03:35.787 --> 00:03:39.514
Command Prompt: not recognized as an internal

82
00:03:39.514 --> 00:03:43.032
or external command. PowerShell:

83
00:03:43.032 --> 00:03:46.376
not recognized as the name of a cmdlet.

84
00:03:46.376 --> 00:03:48.442
Bash and zsh:

85
00:03:48.442 --> 00:03:50.497
command not found.

86
00:03:50.497 --> 00:03:52.670
Same empty street.

87
00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.525
The quieter problem:

88
00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:56.951
several shops can share one name.

89
00:03:56.951 --> 00:04:00.875
Python from an installer, from conda or uv,

90
00:04:00.875 --> 00:04:03.917
and a stand-in from the Microsoft Store.

91
00:04:03.917 --> 00:04:06.251
The courier takes the first.

92
00:04:06.251 --> 00:04:08.875
On this course's Windows machine,

93
00:04:08.875 --> 00:04:11.893
python had three candidates, in this order:

94
00:04:11.893 --> 00:04:15.701
a Python 3.10 install, the Store stand-in,

95
00:04:15.701 --> 00:04:17.849
and a Miniconda Python.

96
00:04:17.849 --> 00:04:21.077
The first one won, and Python 3.10

97
00:04:21.077 --> 00:04:24.084
reached its end of life this month.

98
00:04:24.084 --> 00:04:25.384
Nobody chose that.

99
00:04:25.384 --> 00:04:26.940
The order did.

100
00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:30.051
That Store stand-in is a signpost:

101
00:04:30.051 --> 00:04:31.932
with no Python installed,

102
00:04:31.932 --> 00:04:34.661
it opens the Microsoft Store.

103
00:04:34.661 --> 00:04:37.772
Found on the list doesn't mean installed.

104
00:04:37.772 --> 00:04:41.290
Since Python 3.14, its install manager

105
00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.786
makes the real python a signpost too,

106
00:04:43.786 --> 00:04:47.025
pointing at the install, so check where it leads.

107
00:04:47.025 --> 00:04:48.198
To see who wins,

108
00:04:48.198 --> 00:04:50.230
ask the shell for every candidate,

109
00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:54.167
in order, or ask Python where it lives.

110
00:04:54.617 --> 00:04:58.576
Activating the project's .venv folder pins

111
00:04:58.576 --> 00:05:01.653
its street to the top of the courier's list.

112
00:05:01.653 --> 00:05:04.172
Now python means the project's Python,

113
00:05:04.172 --> 00:05:06.982
because its street comes first.

114
00:05:06.982 --> 00:05:09.536
But only in that one shell,

115
00:05:09.536 --> 00:05:12.055
and the couriers it sends.

116
00:05:12.055 --> 00:05:14.261
Another terminal isn't in it,

117
00:05:14.261 --> 00:05:17.198
and neither is an agent's separate shell.

118
00:05:17.198 --> 00:05:20.565
That's how "I activated it" and "it's using

119
00:05:20.565 --> 00:05:23.282
the wrong Python" can both be true:

120
00:05:23.282 --> 00:05:26.533
you activated it in a different window.

121
00:05:26.533 --> 00:05:29.517
Newer tools skip the activating.

122
00:05:29.517 --> 00:05:32.895
uv run finds the project's environment directly,

123
00:05:32.895 --> 00:05:36.578
and puts it first for just that one command.

124
00:05:37.027 --> 00:05:40.173
You install a tool, and the installer

125
00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:43.877
adds a street to the master copy at home.

126
00:05:43.877 --> 00:05:46.582
But the terminal you had open left home earlier,

127
00:05:46.582 --> 00:05:48.532
carrying the old card.

128
00:05:48.532 --> 00:05:50.854
So it says not recognized.

129
00:05:50.854 --> 00:05:53.490
The fix is to send a new courier:

130
00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:55.579
open a new terminal.

131
00:05:55.579 --> 00:05:57.286
Since 2023,

132
00:05:57.286 --> 00:06:01.013
a new tab in Windows Terminal picks up the change;

133
00:06:01.013 --> 00:06:03.730
a tab that was already open does not.

134
00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.063
A terminal inside an editor gets

135
00:06:06.063 --> 00:06:08.002
its satchel from the editor,

136
00:06:08.002 --> 00:06:11.020
so the editor usually needs a restart.

137
00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:12.681
Agents too.

138
00:06:12.681 --> 00:06:15.978
Claude Code reads your environment when it starts,

139
00:06:15.978 --> 00:06:19.497
so it needs a restart to see a change.

140
00:06:19.948 --> 00:06:21.875
Here is where the picture breaks.

141
00:06:21.875 --> 00:06:24.000
A real satchel can be sealed.

142
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.869
An environment can't.

143
00:06:25.869 --> 00:06:27.204
Any program, script

144
00:06:27.204 --> 00:06:29.991
or agent you start can read every card,

145
00:06:29.991 --> 00:06:33.195
and copies them on to its own couriers.

146
00:06:33.195 --> 00:06:37.433
Some shops on the list are signposts, not shops.

147
00:06:37.433 --> 00:06:39.918
Store and install-manager aliases

148
00:06:39.918 --> 00:06:42.739
and version-manager shims

149
00:06:42.739 --> 00:06:44.573
forward you somewhere else,

150
00:06:44.573 --> 00:06:48.288
so "which" can name the signpost, not the program.

151
00:06:48.288 --> 00:06:50.982
And there is no single environment.

152
00:06:50.982 --> 00:06:55.593
There are as many satchels as running programs.

153
00:06:56.042 --> 00:06:59.223
Now the cards that matter most: keys.

154
00:06:59.223 --> 00:07:01.928
AI tools look for your key on a card

155
00:07:01.928 --> 00:07:03.623
with a known name,

156
00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:07.826
like the Open AI or Anthropic key variable.

157
00:07:07.826 --> 00:07:10.554
That keeps the key out of your code.

158
00:07:10.554 --> 00:07:14.084
Last lesson's .env file is how the key

159
00:07:14.084 --> 00:07:16.522
usually gets into the satchel:

160
00:07:16.522 --> 00:07:19.460
a library loads it, or a runtime:

161
00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.838
Bun on its own, Node when asked.

162
00:07:22.838 --> 00:07:25.578
But inheritance cuts both ways.

163
00:07:25.578 --> 00:07:28.074
A key in your terminal's satchel is copied

164
00:07:28.074 --> 00:07:30.965
into every courier you send from it.

165
00:07:30.965 --> 00:07:32.509
Including an agent,

166
00:07:32.509 --> 00:07:35.412
and every command and package it runs.

167
00:07:35.412 --> 00:07:38.581
If the Anthropic key variable is set,

168
00:07:38.581 --> 00:07:41.240
Claude Code uses that key,

169
00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.225
and its pay-per-use billing,

170
00:07:43.225 --> 00:07:44.967
instead of your subscription,

171
00:07:44.967 --> 00:07:47.033
even when you're logged in.

172
00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:50.168
It asks once in an interactive session,

173
00:07:50.168 --> 00:07:52.281
but never in a scripted run.

174
00:07:52.281 --> 00:07:56.114
Its status screen shows which one is paying.

175
00:07:56.564 --> 00:07:58.689
Five years ago, only programs

176
00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:01.545
you started yourself opened the satchel.

177
00:08:01.545 --> 00:08:05.039
Now agents install tools, edit path,

178
00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:09.254
create environments and write .env files for you,

179
00:08:09.254 --> 00:08:11.820
and they send out couriers of their own.

180
00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:13.817
Attackers noticed.

181
00:08:13.817 --> 00:08:17.520
In August 2025, a poisoned package stole

182
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.551
.env files and steered victims' own AI

183
00:08:20.551 --> 00:08:23.384
command-line tools to hunt for keys;

184
00:08:23.384 --> 00:08:26.391
that autumn, an npm worm harvested

185
00:08:26.391 --> 00:08:28.933
tokens from environment variables.

186
00:08:28.933 --> 00:08:31.011
In December 2025,

187
00:08:31.011 --> 00:08:35.260
npm revoked its old long-lived publishing tokens.

188
00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:38.499
Agent tools now offer ways to strip

189
00:08:38.499 --> 00:08:41.460
keys from what their commands inherit,

190
00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.235
mostly switched off by default.

191
00:08:44.235 --> 00:08:46.673
The direction is to hand a key to one command

192
00:08:46.673 --> 00:08:50.226
for one run, or keep it behind a proxy,

193
00:08:50.226 --> 00:08:53.140
instead of leaving it in every satchel.

194
00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.104
That's still early.

195
00:08:55.553 --> 00:08:57.724
Agents do the setup now.

196
00:08:57.724 --> 00:09:00.522
Your job is to check it, with two questions.

197
00:09:00.522 --> 00:09:03.367
First: which program actually ran?

198
00:09:03.367 --> 00:09:04.516
Ask the shell,

199
00:09:04.516 --> 00:09:07.442
or ask the agent to show you the full path.

200
00:09:07.442 --> 00:09:09.926
Second: where do my keys live,

201
00:09:09.926 --> 00:09:11.412
and who can see them?

202
00:09:11.412 --> 00:09:13.908
Which file, which variable,

203
00:09:13.908 --> 00:09:16.683
which terminal the agent was started from,

204
00:09:16.683 --> 00:09:18.657
and which account pays.

205
00:09:18.657 --> 00:09:21.095
A rule asking an agent not to read

206
00:09:21.095 --> 00:09:23.858
your keys is a request, not a fence.

207
00:09:23.858 --> 00:09:27.423
Real fences, like sandboxes and key scrubbing,

208
00:09:27.423 --> 00:09:30.965
are module fourteen, lesson twelve.

209
00:09:31.415 --> 00:09:33.331
The surprises you'll hit.

210
00:09:33.331 --> 00:09:34.735
You install something,

211
00:09:34.735 --> 00:09:38.509
and the same terminal still says not recognized.

212
00:09:38.509 --> 00:09:39.809
Open a new one.

213
00:09:39.809 --> 00:09:44.209
Typing python opens the Microsoft Store.

214
00:09:44.209 --> 00:09:46.450
That's the stand-in winning.

215
00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:49.724
Python and py can start two different Pythons.

216
00:09:49.724 --> 00:09:53.764
On a Mac, python isn't found, but python3 is.

217
00:09:53.764 --> 00:09:56.388
And on newer Macs, brew isn't found

218
00:09:56.388 --> 00:09:59.522
until Homebrew's folder joins the path.

219
00:09:59.522 --> 00:10:02.878
Pip install worked, but the import fails:

220
00:10:02.878 --> 00:10:05.176
the package went to a different Python

221
00:10:05.176 --> 00:10:07.243
than the one that ran.

222
00:10:07.243 --> 00:10:11.283
An agent's export or activation didn't stick.

223
00:10:11.283 --> 00:10:15.372
In some agents, each command gets a fresh shell.

224
00:10:15.822 --> 00:10:17.807
Safe to ignore for now:

225
00:10:17.807 --> 00:10:20.176
editing the registry by hand,

226
00:10:20.176 --> 00:10:23.043
the full rules for which command wins,

227
00:10:23.043 --> 00:10:25.586
how version-manager shims work,

228
00:10:25.586 --> 00:10:28.245
and environment editor tools.

229
00:10:28.245 --> 00:10:30.149
The neighbours on the map:

230
00:10:30.149 --> 00:10:32.598
shells, in module two.

231
00:10:32.598 --> 00:10:35.687
Many Pythons and virtual environments,

232
00:10:35.687 --> 00:10:37.161
in module five.

233
00:10:37.161 --> 00:10:39.692
Giving agents keys safely,

234
00:10:39.692 --> 00:10:42.467
keeping secrets out of plain text,

235
00:10:42.467 --> 00:10:46.101
and user versus system variables,

236
00:10:46.101 --> 00:10:49.980
in District Five, Trust.

237
00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:52.288
Every program carries a copy

238
00:10:52.288 --> 00:10:54.598
of its parent's environment.

239
00:10:54.598 --> 00:10:57.733
Changes at home only reach programs

240
00:10:57.733 --> 00:11:01.518
started afterwards, so open a new terminal.

241
00:11:01.518 --> 00:11:05.640
Path is an ordered list, and the first match wins.

242
00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:09.564
To know which program ran, ask the shell.

243
00:11:09.564 --> 00:11:12.037
Keys in the environment go wherever

244
00:11:12.037 --> 00:11:16.019
you send programs, agents included.

245
00:11:16.019 --> 00:11:19.666
Know where yours live, and who can read them.
